Vanilla layer order research:
https://github.com/tryashtar/minecraft-version-history/blob/master/personal_config.yaml

Correct the native effect research classification before implementing playback.
The 128-byte timeline records and emitter initialization setter belong to
particles. Timeline sounds contain two hashed strings and a timestamp in
104 bytes. Controller state-entry sound records omit the timestamp.
The target player queues resolved sound names and authored locators after
particle dispatch and before timeline scripts. It uses separate particle
and sound alias tables, rather than a sound table and named event table.

Independent Bedrock 1.26.51.1 execution verifies 14 sound constructor cases,
24 stable timestamp sorts, and 19 sound queue cases. The queue cases cover
missing aliases, bindings and actor context, event boundaries, loops, and
suppression without catch-up. Component lookup and memory-copy boundaries
are supplied by the private harness. These checks establish queue contents,
not audible output, playback locator transforms, parameters, or captions.
Particle probes retain their dispatch and initialization evidence with the
correct labels. No production effect support is claimed by this research.

Normalize assignment and unary operator precedence at the shared Molang
parser boundary before enforcing actor evaluation limits. Existing actor
animation tests and recorded server player controllers validate the
corrected expression semantics.

Native sound consumer and backend

Bedrock 1.26.51.1's sound queue consumer `FUN_146686950` calls
`SoundEngine::playAttached`. Constructor and destructor references identify
the engine vtable. A read-only lookup in the running target client
identifies the backend method.

The position callback `FUN_14668ffd0` checks the actor's weak reference,
registry, entity generation, component, and removal state. A valid locator
supplies its current position. A missing locator uses actor position. An
unavailable actor leaves the supplied properties unchanged. Independent
execution verifies 256 availability, movement, property preservation, and
reference cases. External boundaries supply locator resources and the
locator lookup.

The engine method `FUN_1441c2ff0` resolves the catalog event before it
invokes the backend. A missing event returns the invalid handle without
invoking the position callback. The inspected backend method `FUN_14b3b53a0`
initializes properties to zero and invokes the callback once. It passes the
resulting position to ordinary playback with volume `2`, pitch `1`, and no
server sound handle. It does not retain this callback for later movement.

Independent execution verifies 512 cases through the engine method, backend
method, and actual position callback. The harness supplies catalog lookup
and captures the final playback request. Native code executes the
availability checks, position resolution, defaults, and reference cleanup.
These cases do not produce audible output.

The backend's ordinary playback method selects a sample by its integer
weight. Zero-weight samples receive no selection interval. An event with no
total weight returns the invalid handle. Selected sample volume and pitch
multiply the incoming values. A nonpositional override clears the selected
sample's positional flag. Another 328 native cases verify selection
boundaries, zero weights, multipliers, positional overrides, and handle
allocation. The harness supplies the random draw and stops before sample
loading.

These results establish requests and parameters for the inspected target
backend. They do not establish attenuation, category volume, captions,
audible output, or native multiplayer results. Production still needs effect
resource bindings, locator transforms, dispatch, audio output, and cleanup.
Private executables, memory leads, probes, and licensed assets remain
outside the patch.

Allow authored script fragments within the existing 16,384-character aggregate
budget instead of imposing the 4,096-character bone-expression limit on
each fragment. The saved Bedrock 1.26.51 CubeCraft player script contains
a 6,861-character fragment in a valid bounded aggregate. Retain the
separate smaller limits for bone expressions and execution complexity.

Extend Molang math with inverse_lerp and all 30 easing functions verified
against Bedrock 1.26.51.1's descriptor table and constant-fold dispatcher.
Preserve float operands, extrapolation, exponential endpoint offsets and
the native elastic period. Generate the sine table locally, with no native
installation or copied asset required by production. Private execution
covers 13,578 cases; imported CRT math and allocation boundaries are mocked.

Correct conditional assignment precedence and modern nested conditionals
in the parser. The matching licensed spear script previously retained a
boolean in a duration variable, disabling its interpolation calculations.
Keep Mocha's lexer, AST and evaluator, and bound parser recursion before
applying the existing limits. Private tests observe authored script values
through the actor and bone sampler without changing account recipes.
Live item bindings, first-person rendering and native visible comparison
remain outside this verification.

Resolve vanilla sound catalogs into immutable event and sample parameters after
versioned identifier overrides. Select authored samples with bounded integer
weights and retain native category defaults, sticky is3D state, streaming
flags, captions, raw distances, and legacy attenuation scope. Connect catalog
selection to licensed FSB decoding without a native installation or Java
sound-event substitution.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 executable probes establish the target conversions,
selection, category lookup, and attenuation requests. Private fixtures compare
1,187 native cases and resolve licensed samples against independent PCM
output. Numeric strings and ranges become zero; nonempty strings and
containers are true for sound flags. Reject non-finite parameters and unsafe
weight totals before resource resolution. This resource step does not establish
animation dispatch, audible output, captions, or completed effect parity.

Resolve the licensed catalog's OGG samples as well as FSB banks. Search each
pack's formats before lower packs so an OGG replacement can override FSB.
The target 1.26.51.1 extension initializer declares FSB, OGG, then WAV;
Microsoft documents replacements across those formats. The shared core decoder
now supplies WAV samples through the same playback path.

Use Minecraft's JOrbis decoder after checking container bounds, stream
identity, dimensions, and the final frame count. Bound PCM before allocation
and reject incomplete decoded output. Private libvorbisfile comparisons cover
three licensed and six generated streams with 109,603 scalar samples, within
one PCM quantization step. This verifies decoding rather than audible effects.

Play timeline sounds through each actor's declared resource aliases. Preserve
stable timestamp order and the native previous < time <= current interval.
Suppression consumes the cursor, and loop wraps do not replay skipped cycles.
The target 1.26.51.1 player is independently executed in 19 queue fixtures;
its attached backend snapshots locator or actor position once with gain 2
and pitch 1. An absent alias never becomes a global event-name fallback.

Decode selected FSB, OGG, or WAV assets off the render thread, then create
PCM channels in Minecraft's existing listener and audio device. Bound pending
decodes and active PCM, invalidate requests across audio reload and disconnect,
and release static buffers after their channels. Match category sliders,
source-relative playback, pitch and authored attenuation requests. Streaming
uses the shared stream pool; full decoding still precedes playback. Source
gain above one requires AL_SOFT_gain_clamp_ex. Native audible mixing,
stream concurrency, captions, controller entry effects, particles, dynamic
emote binding and scaled world locator parity remain separate work.

Parse static model locators and sample their bone transforms from the final
pose. Apply the renderer's coordinate basis and fall back to actor position
for absent locators. Tests cover inherited transforms and scale suppression;
these transforms still need an independent native visual comparison.

Accept controller state sound arrays and bind their aliases through the same
actor library as timeline sounds. Run entry and exit scripts only when the
controller's effect gate allows them, while state transitions still consume
the entry. Queue state sounds after sampled children; self transitions reset
players without replaying state effects. Preserve both initial-state format
behaviors and authored source order.

Independent execution of the Bedrock 1.26.51.1 controller update verifies
106 queue and script-callback cases. These include weak binding absence,
actor availability, suppression without catchup, zero-weight controllers,
reentry, self transitions, source order, initial transitions and crossfades.
Child updates and script evaluation are supplied boundaries; actual native
sound queue writes are inspected. Microsoft's controller tutorial confirms
the state array and actor alias shape. Particles and native render-context
gate bindings remain separate work.

Connect animation and controller particle aliases to a bounded world runtime.
Preserve event order and suppression cursors, and capture the source track's
Molang context before timeline scripts run. Support instant and steady
emission, once lifetimes, point/custom offsets, motion, tint gradients,
flipbooks and rectangular camera-facing billboards. Reject other components
explicitly instead of silently approximating their behavior.

Independent execution of Bedrock 1.26.51.1 supplies 200 dynamic-motion and
492 billboard cases. Production kernels match their float32 results, and a
changing-rate test reproduces native steady-rate caching. Native emitter
scheduling, attachment rotation, tint quantization and full visible parity
remain unverified. Collision, curves, local space, other lifetimes, shapes
and facing modes remain separate work. Decode resource images asynchronously
and release textures across account changes and world cleanup.

Apply particle collision after motion using the target 1.26.51.1 swept
bounds algorithm. Native functions 1461c7460 and 1461c80a0 establish the
one-block sweep limit, x/z/y terrain order, slab tie priority, contact
bias, three response passes, restitution, tangential drag and contact
expiration. Independent execution supplies 364 cases; production matches
all position, velocity and terrain-query results exactly as float32.

Query world block collision shapes by cell, including the existing native
custom-block shape overrides. Cache a bounded number of cells per render
frame. Collision events still require the nested particle event runtime.
The oracle supplies terrain boxes and emitter origin accessors; it does
not establish complete native world-shape or visible-effect parity.

Complete emitter shape sampling for box, sphere, disc and entity AABB.
Target 1.26.51.1 functions 1462a6be0, 1462ad0e0, 1462aa600, 1462ab2c0
and 1462a81f0 establish area-weighted surfaces, paired random directions,
disc projection, volume radius, zero-length fallback and normalization.
Point/custom evaluation interleaves offset and direction by axis. Rounded
custom directions retain their authored magnitude at the shape boundary.

Independent native execution supplies 646 cases. Production matches every
position, direction and ordered random call exactly as float32. Random and
entity bounds accessors are supplied boundaries. Thread the actor's current
bounds through timeline/controller requests and each world emission.
Native local-space transforms and visible parity remain separate work.

Native initial-speed function 1461c2690 normalizes the sampled direction
before applying scalar speed. Scalar Molang expressions evaluate separately
for each axis. Vector speed overrides direction. Independent execution
supplies 128 additional cases; production matches every velocity exactly
as float32, including the tiny-direction threshold and signed zeros.

Preserve attached particle matrices instead of translating every newborn
by a live locator point. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 starts attached emitters at zero,
transforms world-space births, and applies local-space matrices at render.
Native 14215b3c0/1419f7910 execute locator selection and actor yaw;
14215a440 establishes birth products, initialization order, and inherited
velocity; 142158a40 establishes origins, cached velocity, and actor loss.

Independent native execution supplies 192 actor matrix cases, 60 birth
cases, 192 emitter updates, and 180 render position cases. Retain float32
operation order, the actor baseline rotation, full locator scale, and
previous-update inherited velocity. Local-position particles clear on
actor loss; world particles drain. Unbound emitters retain their initial
world origin without live actor bounds or local-space transforms.

World attachments use interpolated actor position/body yaw and the current
posed bone matrix. Matrix snapshots preserve their axes through async
playback and world extraction. Authored locator rotation, registry-only
actor resolution, GPU billboard orientation, native visible scale and full
render interpolation remain unverified. Native executables, probe code,
fixtures and screenshots stay private.

Extend the licensed official-package projection to all vanilla texture images and their archives. The saved Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Hive resource stack references vanilla bread, bucket and potion images, while the appearance/effect-only cache omits them. Preserve native path case and every versioned overlay instead of substituting Java images.

Invalidate the previous cache schema and require native item images before publishing the refreshed library. Keep the existing account-bound licensing, checksums, atomic replacement and acquisition limits. Extend helper selection and cache refresh/incomplete-download tests; licensed package bytes remain private.

Bound the raw projection and expanded logical library independently at 256 MiB, retaining the 32 MiB per-file limit. Compressed archive bytes are input rather than additional logical output. The complete matching native image projection contains 18,173 logical files and 211,829,122 bytes; its raw input contains 4,044 files and 149,413,613 bytes. Keep raw input limited to 8,192 files and bound expanded/cache content at 32,768 files instead of discarding native texture layers or increasing byte limits.

Regression tests use small archive payloads to check separate input/output budgets, repeated shared offsets, cache readback beyond the raw-file limit, and both sides of the logical file-count boundary. Targeted provider and asset-cache tests pass: 20 cases pass, with two private licensed acquisition probes skipped.
Retain authored rotation in modern model locators and compose it after the
animated bone transform. Apply ignore_inherited_scale to the final direction
axes, preserving the scaled attachment position. The earlier implementation
removed scale before translation and moved the locator incorrectly.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 locator update 141c362e0 and setter 142088320 establish
rotation composition and post-transform scale suppression. Independent
execution supplies 144 reference cases using resolved bone matrices, stored
radians, preprocessed offsets, conversion factor one, and imported float math.
Tests compare the production kernel and cover modern versus legacy metadata.
Native geometry parsing, world unit conversion, and visible parity remain
outside these fixtures. Licensed fixtures and extraction tools stay private.

Resolve color controls from equipped, loaded persona assets. Bedrock
1.26.51.1's typed metadata callbacks place allow_tint_override at +0x298.
The live factory at 0x1447526e0 copies its optional value to the piece's
+0x410 flag and defaults omitted values to true. Native move construction
preserves this flag. Executing the assignment, move and legacy picker
branches verifies 186 type, loaded-state and optional-flag cases. The
harness supplies decoded optional metadata and the piece-validity
accessor; JSON decoding and other UI implementations remain outside it.

Load only the relevant equipped assets before enabling skin, hair,
facial-hair, mouth and eye controls. Fixed-color or unavailable pieces
have no editable channels. Invalidate asynchronous results after account,
profile or piece changes, and recheck current metadata before a color
write. Other palette callers and free pieces without usable receipt
keys still need separate work.

Resolve color assets before the final profile hash check so a slow licensed
package acquisition cannot leave the save based on an old account recipe.
The save retains the native request shape and exact readback check.

Create the first tint group for an equipped piece without saved colors.
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 picker 14713b490 reaches editor 1486238a0 and
recipe setter 148618b90. That setter initializes all four channels to
transparent black before replacing the chosen channel. Existing groups
retain their other channels. Independent execution covers 240 cases;
the harness observes the native pair at the unordered-map insertion
boundary and supplies existing map state and the security-cookie call.

Enable metadata-approved color controls without requiring a saved group.
Retain malformed-group rejection, palette validation, the final profile
hash check and exact service readback. A live HTTPS test of the production
request builder creates the first iris group and verifies exact readback,
preserving the other profiles and restoring all six original recipes.
The native UI exposes the picker without a group, but its missing preview
keeps Equip disabled. Full native UI save and visible comparisons remain
unverified.

Match saved colors to palette swatches by decoded ARGB value. The target
native account stores opaque colors as either six-digit RGB or eight-digit
ARGB. Raw string comparison loses the selected swatch and enables an
unchanged save. Reuse the tint decoder for selection and Apply state,
preserving the original recipe serialization and transparent alpha.

Read requiresRedeem from the top-level native catalog item and retain paid
offers with their exact Minecoin price. Block recipe writes for unacquired
items while retaining removal of already equipped pieces. The Dressing Room
uses Get for zero-price claims and requires a price confirmation for paid
purchases. Refresh offer and published metadata before the one transaction
POST, then poll only receipt and catalog reads until ownership and the
specific pack key arrive. Do not automatically equip or retry transactions.

A muted Bedrock 1.26.51.1 cold-cache capture establishes the inventory receipt
key source and free emote transaction/virtual flow. Native Secret Handshake
Get adds one entitlement and exposes Equip without changing appearance.
An independent zero-cost Shadow Boxing claim uses the production request
builder with only CorrelationId telemetry. The service accepts it, supplies
ownership and the matching receipt key, and production decrypts its six-file
pack. Transaction IDs are opaque strings. All saved appearances remain
unchanged. Paid transactions, full asynchronous UI acquisition, Xbox Store
redemption, and accounts without receipt keys remain unverified. Captures,
credentials, receipts, assets and probe code stay private.

Synchronize Microsoft Store purchases through transaction/redeem/xbox
before refreshing persona and owned classic catalogs. Use the selected
account's PlayFab-scoped Xbox XSTS token and XUID. The general Xbox Live
token receives HTTP 401; the PlayFab audience is documented by Microsoft's
Store redemption API and accepted by the native Minecraft endpoint.
Validate successful, failed and transaction lists, preserve partial results,
and read the fresh inventory receipt after synchronization. Catalog loading
continues on synchronization failure. Retain visible recipes and pack rows;
disable dependent writes during refresh and discard results after account
or screen changes.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 captures establish MarketplaceData.userId and
xboxAuthentication.XboxToken in the Redemption request. A live production
asynchronous probe synchronizes ten offers with zero failures and zero new
transactions, preserves every appearance and existing receipt key, and
reloads two owned classic packs and the persona catalog. No installed native
client or captured credential is required by production. Visible screen
verification, paid acquisition and receipt-less accounts remain separate
work. Raw captures, tokens and probes stay private.

Support linear, Bezier, Catmull-Rom and Bezier-chain particle curves at
render time before particle render expressions. Keep curve variables
particle-local, evaluate dynamic nodes in native order, and preserve
existing variables for empty chains. Native chain control offsets divide
slopes by three without scaling by the interval duration. Clamp horizontal
ranges to float epsilon and retain float32 boundary arithmetic.

Microsoft documents these curve types at
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/particlesreference/particlecurves
The Bedrock 1.26.51.1 executable supplies evaluators at 1461cb0d0,
1461cd8b0, 1461cdf00 and 1461cd320. Independent execution supplies 196
numeric cases with constant Molang accessors and parsed chain segments.
These fixtures exclude parsing, variable registration, render scheduling
and visible comparison. Private binaries, decompilation and fixtures remain
outside the repository.

Play native collision-triggered child effects so the extracted water-drip
asset can produce its three rain-splash particles. Use the incoming speed
and final contact normal, dispatch before contact expiration, and preserve
the event frame and native event coordinates through child loading.
Support both block expiration predicates using original Bedrock identities
from the tracked primary palette, without an installed native client.

Independent execution of Bedrock 1.26.51.1 supplies 74 collision event cases,
16 event-coordinate cases and 64 block predicate cases. Collision probes
supply terrain, source callbacks and an identity frame template. Block
probes execute the native origin getter and membership lookup with a
prepared block set. Global startup, native registry name resolution,
complete child scheduling and visible comparisons remain unverified.
The production tests load the extracted drip and splash definitions;
licensed assets, binaries and research fixtures remain private.

Play emitter lifecycle and travel events through the bounded child graph.
The target creates events once on the first update, expires before origin
sampling and lifetime reset, and keeps travel distance across loops.
Repeating distance events dispatch once per update, even across several
