The live Prism Flatpak comparison exposed a package-helper startup failure:
the runtime omits WebKitGTK and closes stdin before the license request.
Use flatpak-spawn for the bundled helper when running inside Flatpak,
retain the client display and tie the helper lifetime to its launch.
Host commands require launcher permission; report missing startup receipts
separately from Store account failures without printing process diagnostics.
Read bounded receipt lines while draining output so large diagnostics cannot
hide the final result or deadlock request delivery. Reject success when
the request could not be delivered. Real child-process tests exercise both
pipe directions, oversized diagnostics and early exits. This still uses
our bundled license/extraction helper and never an installed Bedrock game.

The same live Bedrock 1.26.51 comparison acquires the package and assembles
the active Alex model, but its six-digit skcol fails the appearance store's
eight-digit check. Accept both native RGB and ARGB recipe colors without
rewriting them. Verify model, atlas, animation and color persistence and
SkinData propagation for both lengths, including a return to classic skin.

The target 1.26.51.1 native Bases request exposes the equipped Brawny Skin
as price zero with requiresRedeem, without a pack-specific receipt key.
Its published PersonaDurable identity matches and the production decoder
reads its body and face tint maps using existing account receipt keys.
Treat zero-price asset eligibility separately from redemption permission
to equip; keep published price, content type, product and pack checks.
Require receipt keys and never redeem or spend currency while preparing
an already saved recipe. Parameterize the identity guards across both
redemption states and retain the unclaimed equipment restriction.

Resolve color-control metadata through the same persona asset reference
factory as model assembly. An equipped zero-price skin that still carries
a redemption flag can use existing account receipt keys. Equipping an
unclaimed item remains separately restricted; loading color controls
performs no transaction.

Remove the outdated UI claim that saved characters exclude animations;
the assembled appearance now preserves its animated surfaces.

Use Minecraft's named left mouse button for wardrobe tiles. Minecraft 26.3 uses button 1, so the previous zero checks rejected real left clicks even though footer widgets worked. Make palette swatches native button widgets for mouse input, keyboard navigation, and narration. Fit their rows and color status above the footer at the current GUI size, without overlapping Apply or clipping the selected color.

Keep all six native face labels when expanding a cape's box UVs into
explicit faces for the persona atlas. The shared geometry consumer now
converts both layouts consistently, so swapping either lateral or vertical
labels in the producer would apply the coordinate conversion twice.
Preserve the native corner orientation: reverse both UV axes on the upper
face and only V on the lower face. The target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 parser
binds up/down to native faces 1/0; the rendering feature records the exact
versioned parser and normal-table evidence.

The existing licensed native cape oracle checks positions, normals,
texture coordinates, and atlas pixels at all four persona heights.
It passes unchanged together with the box/per-face geometry tests.

Ship an independent headless package helper for Flatpak runtimes that lack
WebKitGTK. Keep interactive Store sign-in as the default host feature;
headless acquisition requires valid Store credentials and preserves token
expiry, selected-account, and fresh device-bound license validation.
Missing or expired credentials return an explicit sign-in requirement.

Consume bounded private helper output before reporting an early process
exit, so loader errors do not become misleading closed-stdin failures.
Keep payloads and credentials out of diagnostics, and package both helper
variants with independent checksums for local builds and release CI.

Expose Default for arms and legs without requiring users to find each
equipped offer. Clear only the selected limb side through the existing
profile hash guard and account readback. Share the side masking logic
with piece replacement so free-source flags, tints and the opposite
side survive both paths.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 removes the right Coin Stacks leg visually when
Default is selected for Right, leaving the left leg visible on return
to the Dressing Room. Its local recipe cache still records both legs;
this observation does not establish native persistence or cloud sync.
The Java account operation explicitly saves and verifies its recipe.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Dressing Room previews play locked Sneaking and
unowned Robot Dance without a purchase or recipe save. Robot Dance sends
GetPublishedItem for its Store product, then downloads personabinary from
an official CDN. The existing current-account receipt decodes that archive
and its 7.5-second animation. Allow catalog emote previews and received
emotes from the full current emote catalog, while retaining separate
ownership checks for equipping. Validate product, persona type, pack UUID,
and archive identity before using receipt keys. No receipt, asset, key,
third-party service, or native-install path is embedded in the add-on.
Resolve remote UUIDs absent from the current Dressing Room page through
PlayFab Catalog/Search by the pack UUID tag. Native-session requests and
automated current-account requests confirm the target master-player token
returns the product while the title-player token returns an empty result.
Reject mismatched and ambiguous search results, then refresh published
metadata and decode with current receipt keys. Bound pending downloads to
eight and cached emotes to 128 while retaining current wheel assets.

Live 1280-by-694 wardrobe validation exposed an unowned paid offer labeled
available to equip and a three-row emote wheel overlapping the footer.
Use catalog equip eligibility for the ownership label and reserve the
wheel's complete height before selecting its diamond layout. Shorter
windows use one row. Align the position label with the actual controls.
Recalculate the catalog page from the selected item on resize so the
visible tile and its detail remain associated.

Hover's current official animation uses query.life_time for its steady
hover. In inspected Bedrock 1.26.51.1, the actor-bound query clock reads
(actor age ticks + frame alpha) / 20 and preserves monotonic lifetime.
A native instruction probe reproduces 53 actor and preview clock cases.
The documentation has a reported lifetime/animation-time discrepancy:
https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/minecraft-creator/issues/772

World emotes now receive the same actor query context as the player
animation graph, instead of substituting elapsed emote time for lifetime.
Keep the animation's elapsed clock separate. Remove unused sampling
overloads so every world caller supplies its entity context. Verify Hover
root position and rotation across independently varied clocks using
licensed assets. Preview clock and native world phase remain separate
verification work.

Match the native 1.26.51.1 wallet placement throughout the Dressing Room.
Read Minecoins from the captured empty POST balance endpoint with the
Minecraft session, keep other currencies separate, and reject missing or
invalid amounts. Refresh independently on account changes, game focus,
acquisitions, and Store synchronization. The plus button opens Minecraft's
official Minecoin shop because Fabric cannot use the native platform IAP.
No native installation or purchase credentials are needed for this flow.

Client entities can declare effects without animations or scripts. Keep
those aliases when optional sections are absent so effect-only server
player overrides still load. Matching vanilla entities establish the
optional-section behavior; ordinary vanilla player assets declare no
emote effect aliases and do not require a fabricated fallback graph.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1's classic loader gates cape metadata on a trusted pack
callback and Package (2) or PremiumCache (8) origin. A live startup trace
records both actual callbacks returning true. Accept bundled cape PNGs
only after the account receipt, published pack identity, and content
validation. Local imports stay untrusted. Match the native 1023-byte path
limit and slash/backslash basename lookup. Keep decoded pixels and a stable
cape identity through preview, account-scoped storage, preset selection,
and skin transport. Preserve manual PNG imports as an independent local
extension; native precedence against account/persona capes is unverified.

Complete valid wallet requests even while the shop confirmation covers
the screen, so returning cannot leave the counter pending indefinitely.
Account and request identity still discard stale responses.

Bind authored bone pivots and the render tick fraction in production
player and preview animation queries. Preserve absolute native pixels
before hierarchy transforms; accept native numeric and named axes.
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 callback execution covers valid, missing, and malformed
pivot requests and confirms that frame alpha passes through unchanged.
This supplies required graph inputs; native first-person hand and item
submission still needs its separate renderer integration.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 HTTPS captures establish native classic
selection: preserve persona settings and recipes, set skin=true and cs_arm,
and append the manifest UUID plus original skins.json index. Persist the
manifest UUID plus skin localization name for local and wire skin identity.
Save owned classic selections through appearance receipts and fresh readback.
Load active account classic references from receipt-authorized owned packs
instead of rendering their retained persona recipe. Preserve emote wheels
before classic references, and return to persona mode when editing its body.

Track native walking distance per player and tick, including remote players.
Java accumulates scaled distance per local move call and cannot supply this
actor query. Native 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 adds horizontal tick travel,
extrapolates current and previous distances with frame alpha, then scales
by 0.6. Preserve native float arithmetic and keep limb swing independent.
Executable probes cover the real component update and query callbacks,
including a 1,000-tick accumulation regression. First-person graph drawing
and its camera/item transforms still require renderer integration.

Acquire render-controller JSON and archives from the licensed Bedrock
1.26.51.1 package. Refresh versioned caches that lack these resources.
Evaluate primary-model part visibility after animation sampling in the
same Molang scope, including JSON booleans and ordered wildcard rules.
Separate first-person playback from body playback so hand queries cannot
switch the world graph or repeat its sounds and particles. Apply the
licensed hand and sleeve selection inside Java's hand submission with
exception-safe scope restoration. Persona skins also load the native
graph. Native arm drawing transforms, held-item transforms, and full
first-person motion comparisons remain incomplete.

Bind first-person equip heights and authored arm offsets before graph sampling.
Native 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 renderFirstPerson at 0x1447ca170
interpolates each hand's height independently, stores offhand height in
context, and subtracts each authored arm pivot Y from 22. Keep these
values separate from Java's inverse swap transform. Private executable
probes cover 343 height pairs and 100 arm-pivot pairs. This supplies graph
inputs; native camera and item drawing still require integration.

Keep first-person target rotation queries in the native zero-rotation scope
while preserving real camera pitch in v.player_x_rotation. The target
renderFirstPerson clears ActorRotationComponent storage, MobBodyRotationComponent
(FNV32 d7f64bba), and ActorHeadRotationComponent (FNV32 babe7211) before
actor dispatch, then restores them. Native pitch callback 0x14065a550
executes against these cleared values in 567 private probe cases.
Do not mutate world or preview queries when binding the hand frame.

Match native entity-relative bone matrices, including inherited scale and
shear. Target BoneOrientation::updateBoneTransform at 0x141bc3d20
translates the origin through the parent, then clears its entire basis
before applying the bone rotation and scale. Preserve authored local
rotation rather than ancestor bind rotation. Private executable probes
cover 840 matrix combinations, including singular parents. Render and
bounds scopes apply full model-space matrices without inverting the
parent, and restore nesting and exceptions.

Replace Java's fixed camera-angle hand sway with the target native rotation
spring for appearances with native first-person playback. Keep its angular
velocity, damping, integration, wrapped samples, and clock on each appearance.
The native bob option also controls this spring, and scoped submission keeps
ordinary Java rendering unchanged when no native frame is available.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 runs this arithmetic in 0x1404c4400.
Private executable probes cover 435 cases and 948 retained frames at four
frame schedules. Projection, walking bob, first-person graph drawing, and
held-item transforms remain separate work.

Reproduce target-build PlayerBob smoothing, swimming and ground gates,
riding reset, airborne Mob tilt, and first-person view translation and
rotation. Native 1.26.51.1 functions 14900c6c0, 14900e360, 1401ef976 and
1446bd990 establish the rules. The bob coefficient is 0.08, the sideways
factor is 0.65, and airborne tilt uses atan(-velocityY * 0.2) * 15 with
0.4 smoothing. Share the native sine lookup with Molang expressions.
Only replace the first-person bob when the submitted skin has a native
actor graph; preserve Java world bob and the ordinary-player fallback.
Velocity remains the translated Java player's motion, and native camera
projection and complete hand/item geometry submission remain separate.

Use the ordinary native hand projection from Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build
51061372. The first-person caller at 0x1404c470b disables the world-FOV
branch of 0x1446bd180. Its base is 70 degrees, with water-only narrowing
and an uncapped death curve gated by the native FOV-effects toggle.
The native signed-short death getter at 0x142082df0 supplies the counter.
Reuse Java's advancing deathTime with that conversion instead of adding
a second clock. Treat a positive Java FOV-effects scale as the native
boolean toggle. The native projection block at 0x1404c4710 uses a 0.025
near plane. Keep Java's reversed depth and GPU depth range through its
Projection API. Restrict the hook to matching native actor appearances
and first-person views. The separate DeathCameraComponent controller,
native hand geometry submission, and visible motion comparison still
need integration and verification.

Check existing assets and Store credentials before requesting interactive login. Default Microsoft Store sign-in to Ask, with the standard in-game confirmation screen before opening the helper. Remember consent or refusal per account for this game session and share pending consent across concurrent requests. Escape and screen removal decline the request. Allow and Never remain explicit settings. Preserve the typed missing-credentials result for callers that can continue without licensed assets.

Check missing Store credentials before the headless helper fetches a package index or enrolls a device. Background previews must return the structured sign-in requirement promptly. Keep token storage and locking shared with the normal acquisition flow; a headless regression proves this exits before an invalid package URL can reach network validation.

Fresh Linux enrollment revealed that Xodus invokes pkexec to read
SMBIOS before opening its login window. Use the same protocol components
for unavailable hardware without elevation, retaining independent device
credentials and device-bound licensing. Propagate enrollment failures
instead of panicking. Start login after the pinned package header check
and before index traversal to avoid delaying the authentication window.
Other platforms keep their native unprivileged hardware probes.

Initialize an unselected Dressing Room from the account's active character.
Keep local selections made during downloads, and preserve offline defaults
when account assets cannot be acquired.

Reuse the Bedrock app's MSA refresh token for the Microsoft licensing scope.
Verify its Xbox identity before licensing the pinned package. Linux 1.26.51.1
captures obtain its license and extract 6,146 files from fresh device state
without an interactive Store login. Pass short-lived tokens through stdin,
keep license keys inside the helper, and retain Ask for interactive fallback.
Run the headless helper inside Flatpak so silent acquisition does not require
host-command permission. The embedded webview still needs its host runtime.

Use MinecraftAuth's device-code flow under the selected Bedrock app identity
for consented Store reauthentication. Open the system browser and keep the
code, reopen, copy-link, and cancel actions in game. Poll for up to fifteen
minutes on a cancellable worker; cancel that worker without interrupting
optional resource-pack preparation. Bind the fresh Xbox ticket to the
selected account before retaining its MSA session.

Remove the native WebView login and its platform UI dependencies. One
helper per platform now performs verified licensing and extraction inside
the launcher environment, including Flatpak, without host execution or
WebKit. Retain existing licensed Store sessions for cache compatibility.

Native server sound commands use the shared PCM backend and the core's
versioned payload. Preserve handles, pending control order, finite and
infinite loop counts, playback offsets, volume and pitch changes, fades,
seeking, and independent server/game pause state. Stop replaced handles
and cancel pending work on disconnect or audio-device teardown. Keep
server audio independent of emote cleanup.

Resolve native definitions and samples from the converted resource pack
so ViaProxy uses the same playback path. Reuse already licensed assets
without initiating sign-in from an audio callback. If those assets are
unavailable, decode the mapped Java sample and preserve playback controls.

PCM streams repeat whole frames without copying the sample for each loop.
Track processed OpenAL buffers so seeking preserves remaining loop counts
instead of counting prefetched frames as played. Bound queued controls and
use generation checks to discard work from a replaced resource context.

The pinned native capture establishes packet layouts and API availability.
Audible native comparisons, listener-range eligibility, captions, and
stream interruption policies remain separate verification requirements.

Resolve WAV replacements in the native FSB, OGG, WAV order within each pack,
so a higher pack still overrides every lower format. Dispatch RIFF samples
to the shared ViaBedrock decoder and include WAV in server effect resources.
This keeps custom server audio independent of Store sign-in and uses the same
converted-pack resource transport for direct and ViaProxy connections.
WAV loop metadata and multichannel playback remain incomplete.

Retain loose WAV files in licensed vanilla sound extraction, matching the
pinned native sound initializer's FSB, Ogg, and WAV candidates. Advance
the extraction cache format so earlier file selections do not survive
the broader allowlist. Resource path and byte limits remain enforced.

Apply the core native request range gate against the client's audio
listener transform before registering server handles or decoding samples.
The native 1.26.51.1 request callback rejects boundary and distant requests
before dispatch, independent of sample is3D, category, or attenuation.
An ineligible request must not replace an existing handle. Using the audio
listener also preserves camera-controlled placement on direct and proxy
connections without attempting to reconstruct the client camera in core.

Add server closed-caption metadata lookup and a client HUD driven by core
caption state. Capture player/listener pose when a request passes the native
range gate. Keep captions independent of sample decoding and channel
allocation. Resolve server-pack and already licensed caption translations
without initiating Store authentication. Clear queued captions on disconnect.

Match the pinned native caption controls: disabled by default, separate own
and ambient filters, top/bottom right placement, and 1,000 to 4,000 milliseconds
in half-second steps, with a 1,500 millisecond default. The native settings
factory and duration-vector initializer establish those values. Native HUD
resources define a 30% area, 50-unit inset, directional arrows, and a final
one-second in-quart fade. Exact font/layout parity and local actor caption
sources remain unverified or incomplete.

Retain texts/*.lang and texts archives in the independent licensed loader.
Require the English catalog in cache format 10 so an older extraction cannot
silently provide a translation-incomplete resource library. Keep server text
transport separate from the existing audio archive for reader compatibility.

Resolve particle level-sound parameters before PCM loading, apply core's
native local-emitter range gate to the original float position, and dispatch
unmarked captions from the resolved catalog metadata. Native 1.26.51.1's
particle sound alias path calls this emitter with marker zero. Captions
must not disappear merely because a sample is missing or no channel opens.
The own-marker probe separately establishes the actor identity branch; it
does not yet complete network actor sound transport or animation captions.

Share accepted server resource snapshots across native audio and particle
consumers. Decode the versioned particle archive through the core codec,
merge resource kinds by their original pack indexes, and combine them
only with already loaded licensed assets. Missing archives and absent
Store accounts do not initiate asset acquisition or prevent joining.

Reject mismatched indexes, duplicate paths and aggregate decoded limits.
Invalidate shared snapshots on resource, account and world transitions.
Native particle packet dispatch and ViaProxy block identity remain pending.

Use the core's native block identity mirror for particle expiration on
both direct and ViaProxy connections. The client adapter binds received
snapshots to its current world and clears them on disconnect. Resource
reloads preserve the world mirror while releasing particle assets.
Remove the direct connection requirement for native emitter creation;
filtered emitters still require initialized authoritative block access.
Packet 118 dispatch and native visible comparisons remain incomplete.

Receive server particle requests over the advertised native playback channel.
Use accepted server archives and cached licensed assets without opening Store
authentication. Preserve typed scalar and struct variables, deep-copy struct
assignments, and bind actor IDs with UUID and world lifecycle checks. Fall back
to the complete Java packet when an effect cannot start.

Target 1.26.51.1 executable probes establish float32 conversion, case-sensitive
UTF-8 member hashing, ordered member lookup, and distinct duplicate root/member
rules. Packet playback and general actor queries still require live comparison.

Use MochaFloats 6.0.2 interpreter modules and FloatExpression nodes for
Bedrock's single-precision arithmetic. Preserve the native easing
extensions, typed variable map, query bindings, modern precedence and
loop budgets. The engine no longer loads an unused bytecode compiler.
Match the ViaBedrock dependency so the client and proxy use one runtime.

Numeric regressions cover rounding before comparison, persistent
assignments and query values, including the documented 16.7-million
integer boundary. The fork uses the org.redlance namespace:
https://github.com/PlayerAnimationLibrary/mochafloats/tree/1a5dc0fc18bb7f8e7237c0de9cb66299362048ab

Use core's shared native Molang math and trigonometry for client animation
and view bobbing. Remove the duplicate client math library. Preserve
finite scalar results instead of replacing values above 32,768 with zero;
the target's runtime preserves these values before consumer transforms.
Non-finite rejection and parser/execution limits remain in place.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 constant and runtime angle/interpolation operations match
across 9,367 private native cases, including directed endpoints, half-turn
ties, extrapolation, large finite values, and overflow intermediates.
Core carries the unchanged native easing functions and their regenerated
sine lookup. These numeric comparisons do not prove visible animation.

Receive core actor input snapshots through the negotiated actor_state
channel on both direct and ViaProxy routes. Resolve the local frontend
avatar independently of its Bedrock login UUID. Install only in the
captured Minecraft connection and cleanup generation on the client thread.
Replace retained PlayerEntity object ownership with shared native lifetime
tokens for charging, spell color and attached animation effects.

Remove the direct-only actor-state mixin and connection-owned mirror.
The shared registry preserves typed properties, flags, variants, scale
and runtime identity for native rendering queries without a synthetic
UserConnection. Tests retain the native charging and spell-color fixtures
and cover updates, incarnation replacement, stale removal, local identity
and cleanup. Full animation, actor event and lifetime comparisons remain
open; this change transports existing authoritative inputs.

Draw the sampled first-person actor and persona surfaces through their complete
bone hierarchies before Java item transforms. The matching native 1.26.51.1
renderFirstPerson path draws the actor at zero position with cleared rotation;
its actor root subtracts half the collision height and applies model scale,
axis conversion, and a 1/128-block vertical offset. Preserve separate hand-view
models and clocks, authored visibility, and parent transforms. Avoid resetting
individual arms and applying Java hand transforms a second time.

Native executable probes cover root matrices, signed script scale, collapsed
scale and skipped offsets. This drawing path uses the normal actor scale.
Script scale, global actor animations, server costume selection and native
held-item transforms remain separate integration work. Visible comparison is
required before claiming first-person parity.

Apply target first-person death and exact raw-name global transforms before
the axis flip and script scale. Native applyGlobalAnims at 0x141d5d280 and
its rotation helper at 0x141d5d070 suppress gliding/riptide for the local
first-person player property set by 0x1447ca170. Private execution covers
415 global matrices, while 354 existing root matrices retain the outer
composition order. Core supplies raw NAME metadata without format or case
changes. Native death-clock lifecycle, gamertag initialization and visible
parity remain unverified; Java supplies the current death counter.

Keep render-controller selection beside the shared actor graph. Bind a
selection to the sampled playback scope so perspective variables, animation
assignments, and scale expressions remain visible without rerunning actor
scripts. Include original skin surfaces when the drawing pass requests them,
and preserve independent controller visibility, texture, UV, and lighting.
Server costumes and equipped models use the same selector in later patches.

Use local eye height when submitting first-person actor geometry. Live
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 posed bone matrices already contain the same 24-pixel Y
bind origin as ModelPart. Remove the extra translation that counted this
origin twice. The earlier corner check omitted the native bone transform.
Portable comparisons now apply captured bone and actor matrices to all cube
corners. Direct and ViaProxy hand-entry probes match the layered right-arm
[8,15,10] and left-arm [1,18,-1] translations and scale 0.875. Projection,
final visible placement, and pose-dependent offset lifecycle remain open.

Resolve licensed player descriptions with the core inheritance merger too. Keep per-pack duplicate identifier rejection and return independent merged data. The matching official asset layers declare 68 aliases in the last player document but retain four earlier aliases under native layer semantics. Resource-defined playback now keeps omitted roots and script fields while honoring upper overrides and explicit empty root lists. Matched 1280x694 native and Java fixture masks place both hands within one pixel; lighting and broader movement/item parity remain separate requirements.

Bind first-person item queries to the stacks retained by the hand renderer during item swaps. Keep equipped hands and active-use durations separate from rendered hands. Minecraft 26.3 supplies the retained stacks in FirstPersonHandsAndItemsRenderState; target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 item-name callback execution and the official query contract distinguish these sources. Test expression-driven arm poses over retained, replaced, and empty rendered stacks without modifying the actor frame.

The target hand tick raises matching hands toward height one and lowers animated replacements toward zero with a 0.4 step. Native instruction execution covers 900 independent two-hand cases. Remove the Java attack-cooldown multiplier only while a loaded native graph matches the local skin texture; retain ordinary Java fallback and the existing swap classifier. Native item identity classification, busy-hand behavior, replacement thresholds, and full visible timing still need separate comparison.

Bind query.blocking to the core actor BLOCKING flag instead of Java's
item-use state. The licensed Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 callback
reads flag 72 directly, including during TRANSITION_BLOCKING. Preserve
Java's fallback only when no native actor snapshot exists. Existing core
state transport supplies the flag on direct and ViaProxy connections.

Cover actor replacement, stale removal, and registry cleanup. An optional
private fixture compares all 64 native callback cases against the binding.

Bind query.shield_blocking_bob to the core player blocking timeline.
Advance the registry once per active Minecraft simulation tick, preserving
paused clocks and clearing all timing state with the connection. Core
retains the timestamp guards, rising edges, lifetime checks, unsigned
elapsed conversion, and float calculation. The add-on supplies the tick
hook and rendering query only; both connection routes use existing native
actor snapshots.

The matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 callback checks the local
blocking-start timestamp but subtracts the damaged-shield timestamp.
Actual instruction fixtures cover 120 start branches, 1728 damage branches,
and 2700 bob calculations. Licensed memory probes and protocol captures
remain private. Full shield pose and swap parity require separate work.

Use the shared native hand update for matching loaded appearance graphs.
Replace the cooldown-only hook with a complete height and retained-copy
lifecycle: preserve prior heights, omit Java hands-busy suppression, and
copy each retained stack at the native inclusive 0.1 boundary. Keep
ordinary Java updates when the graph is absent, pending, failed, or mismatched.

Item comparison still uses Java's selection rules and remains an explicit
gap. Do not claim native item classification or full visible swap parity.
Core supplies target arithmetic verified against Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build
51061372. Rendering integration is the only frontend-specific work.

Exclude opaque native stack transport context from Java fallback item
comparisons. Preserve all other Java components and existing native
identity and animation markers. The incoming and retained stacks keep
complete native comparison data; only the fallback comparison views omit
it. Context-only updates must not create extra swap animations before the
native item classifiers replace the fallback rules.
